1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,251
A snake biting its own tail.

2
00:00:02,251 --> 00:00:04,465
A round table sharing one big plate.

3
00:00:04,465 --> 00:00:06,302
Mothballs with a strong smell.

4
00:00:06,702 --> 00:00:08,876
A ring, a shared plate, a smell.

5
00:00:08,876 --> 00:00:11,183
All three point to one famous molecule.

6
00:00:11,633 --> 00:00:13,453
Aarav sees it while studying.

7
00:00:13,453 --> 00:00:16,547
He draws benzene as a ring
with three double bonds.

8
00:00:16,897 --> 00:00:20,495
Double bonds should add
bromine fast, like an alkene.

9
00:00:20,495 --> 00:00:23,106
But his book says benzene hardly does.

10
00:00:23,106 --> 00:00:24,407
It swaps instead.

11
00:00:24,907 --> 00:00:28,125
Why does benzene refuse to
act like three double bonds?

12
00:00:28,125 --> 00:00:30,521
By the end, you will know its secret.

13
00:00:31,171 --> 00:00:32,574
So what is benzene?

14
00:00:32,924 --> 00:00:34,840
Benzene is C six H six.

15
00:00:34,840 --> 00:00:38,857
Michael Faraday first isolated
it in eighteen twenty five.

16
00:00:39,207 --> 00:00:41,501
Six carbons and only six hydrogens.

17
00:00:41,501 --> 00:00:45,098
So it should be very unsaturated,
full of double bonds.

18
00:00:45,448 --> 00:00:46,921
Yet it behaves calmly.

19
00:00:46,921 --> 00:00:50,560
It prefers substitution, swapping
a hydrogen, over addition.

20
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:54,977
Benzene and its relatives are called
aromatic compounds, or arenes.

21
00:00:55,377 --> 00:00:57,711
Aromatic first meant sweet smelling.

22
00:00:57,711 --> 00:01:00,571
Today it means a ring
with extra stability.

23
00:01:01,221 --> 00:01:04,005
Let's build benzene on our molecule bench.

24
00:01:04,355 --> 00:01:08,705
In eighteen sixty five, Kekule drew
a ring of six carbons, with single

25
00:01:08,705 --> 00:01:10,673
and double bonds taking turns.

26
00:01:11,073 --> 00:01:13,451
But there is a second way to draw it.

27
00:01:13,451 --> 00:01:16,324
Move every double bond
one place along the ring.

28
00:01:16,724 --> 00:01:18,826
Neither drawing alone is true.

29
00:01:18,826 --> 00:01:22,786
The real molecule is a blend
of both, a resonance hybrid.

30
00:01:23,186 --> 00:01:24,441
X rays prove it.

31
00:01:24,441 --> 00:01:28,898
Benzene is flat, and all six carbon
carbon bonds are equal, one hundred

32
00:01:28,898 --> 00:01:30,688
and thirty nine picometres.

33
00:01:31,088 --> 00:01:35,838
That sits between a single bond, at one
hundred and fifty four, and a double

34
00:01:35,838 --> 00:01:38,400
bond, at one hundred and thirty three.

35
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:42,764
So we often draw benzene as a
hexagon with a circle inside.

36
00:01:42,764 --> 00:01:45,829
The circle means the
bonds are shared equally.

37
00:01:46,479 --> 00:01:47,981
Now the orbital picture.

38
00:01:47,981 --> 00:01:52,063
Each carbon is s p two hybridised,
with angles of one hundred and twenty

39
00:01:52,063 --> 00:01:52,963
degrees.

40
00:01:53,094 --> 00:01:56,471
It makes sigma bonds with
two carbons and one hydrogen.

41
00:01:56,471 --> 00:01:58,750
All of them lie flat in one plane.

42
00:01:59,150 --> 00:02:03,518
Each carbon also keeps one p orbital,
standing straight up and down.

43
00:02:03,918 --> 00:02:05,372
Here is the trick.

44
00:02:05,372 --> 00:02:09,555
Each p orbital overlaps with
both of its neighbours, equally.

45
00:02:09,955 --> 00:02:12,837
So the six pi electrons do
not stay in three pairs.

46
00:02:12,837 --> 00:02:15,878
They spread around the whole
ring, above and below it.

47
00:02:15,878 --> 00:02:17,535
This is called delocalisation.

48
00:02:17,935 --> 00:02:20,793
Like food on a round table
that everyone shares.

49
00:02:20,793 --> 00:02:23,266
Shared electrons make
benzene extra stable.

50
00:02:23,666 --> 00:02:27,059
So benzene would rather swap a
hydrogen than break its ring.

51
00:02:27,459 --> 00:02:29,224
So which rings are aromatic?

52
00:02:29,224 --> 00:02:31,414
Huckel's rule, one link at a time.

53
00:02:31,764 --> 00:02:32,664
Link one.

54
00:02:32,449 --> 00:02:34,129
The molecule must be a ring.

55
00:02:34,879 --> 00:02:35,779
Link two.

56
00:02:35,609 --> 00:02:39,232
The ring must be flat, so all
the p orbitals stand parallel.

57
00:02:39,982 --> 00:02:40,882
Link three.

58
00:02:40,807 --> 00:02:45,023
Every ring atom must give a p orbital,
so the pi electrons can travel all

59
00:02:45,023 --> 00:02:46,036
the way round.

60
00:02:46,786 --> 00:02:47,686
Link four.

61
00:02:47,595 --> 00:02:49,118
Count the pi electrons.

62
00:02:49,118 --> 00:02:53,873
The count must be four n plus two,
where n is zero, one, two and so on.

63
00:02:53,873 --> 00:02:55,917
So two, six, ten, fourteen.

64
00:02:56,667 --> 00:03:00,262
Benzene has three double
bonds, so six pi electrons.

65
00:03:00,262 --> 00:03:02,704
Six is four times one, plus two.

66
00:03:02,704 --> 00:03:03,604
Aromatic.

67
00:03:04,237 --> 00:03:05,137
Now compare.

68
00:03:04,962 --> 00:03:07,263
Cyclobutadiene is a ring
with two double bonds.

69
00:03:07,263 --> 00:03:08,645
That is four pi electrons.

70
00:03:09,045 --> 00:03:10,230
Pause and think.

71
00:03:10,230 --> 00:03:14,180
Naphthalene is two benzene rings
joined, with ten pi electrons.

72
00:03:14,180 --> 00:03:15,365
Aromatic or not?

73
00:03:18,565 --> 00:03:19,465
Aromatic!

74
00:03:19,154 --> 00:03:20,992
Ten is four times two, plus two.

75
00:03:20,992 --> 00:03:24,210
Four pi electrons, like
cyclobutadiene, does not fit the rule.

76
00:03:24,861 --> 00:03:25,886
Remember this.

77
00:03:25,886 --> 00:03:29,635
Flat ring, electrons all the
way round, four n plus two.

78
00:03:30,335 --> 00:03:31,860
Now, one worked example.

79
00:03:31,860 --> 00:03:33,990
Is the cyclopentadienyl anion aromatic?

80
00:03:34,290 --> 00:03:35,190
Step one.

81
00:03:35,072 --> 00:03:37,096
It is a ring of five carbons.

82
00:03:37,096 --> 00:03:38,022
Ring: yes.

83
00:03:38,772 --> 00:03:39,672
Step two.

84
00:03:39,621 --> 00:03:44,552
All five carbons are s p two, so the
ring is flat, and every carbon has

85
00:03:44,552 --> 00:03:45,620
a p orbital.

86
00:03:46,370 --> 00:03:47,270
Step three.

87
00:03:47,206 --> 00:03:48,460
Count pi electrons.

88
00:03:48,460 --> 00:03:50,187
Two double bonds give four.

89
00:03:50,187 --> 00:03:53,286
The negative charge is a
lone pair in a p orbital.

90
00:03:53,286 --> 00:03:54,640
That gives two more.

91
00:03:55,390 --> 00:03:56,302
Step four.

92
00:03:56,302 --> 00:03:57,981
Four plus two is six.

93
00:03:57,981 --> 00:04:01,558
Six equals four n plus
two, with n equal to one.

94
00:04:02,308 --> 00:04:05,806
So the cyclopentadienyl anion
is aromatic, just like benzene.

95
00:04:06,456 --> 00:04:08,394
How do we make benzene in the lab?

96
00:04:08,794 --> 00:04:09,694
One.

97
00:04:09,236 --> 00:04:13,220
Pass ethyne through a red hot iron
tube at eight hundred and seventy three

98
00:04:13,220 --> 00:04:14,120
kelvin.

99
00:04:13,793 --> 00:04:15,755
Three molecules join into a ring.

100
00:04:16,155 --> 00:04:17,055
Two.

101
00:04:16,681 --> 00:04:19,166
Heat sodium benzoate with soda lime.

102
00:04:19,166 --> 00:04:22,198
The COO group leaves as sodium carbonate.

103
00:04:22,198 --> 00:04:23,879
This is decarboxylation.

104
00:04:24,279 --> 00:04:25,179
Three.

105
00:04:24,903 --> 00:04:27,684
Pass phenol vapour over heated zinc dust.

106
00:04:27,684 --> 00:04:31,048
Zinc takes away the oxygen,
and benzene is left.

107
00:04:31,448 --> 00:04:35,268
And on a large scale, benzene
is taken out of coal tar.

108
00:04:35,918 --> 00:04:37,379
Now, the exam trap.

109
00:04:37,729 --> 00:04:41,329
Students say any ring with double
bonds taking turns is aromatic.

110
00:04:41,329 --> 00:04:44,471
So cyclooctatetraene, with
four double bonds, must be.

111
00:04:44,871 --> 00:04:45,771
Wrong.

112
00:04:45,441 --> 00:04:49,940
It has eight pi electrons, which is
not four n plus two, and its ring is

113
00:04:49,940 --> 00:04:50,840
not flat.

114
00:04:50,707 --> 00:04:52,400
It is shaped like a tub.

115
00:04:52,400 --> 00:04:53,354
Not aromatic.

116
00:04:54,004 --> 00:04:55,305
Where is this asked?

117
00:04:55,305 --> 00:04:57,812
Huckel's rule, and bond
lengths in benzene.

118
00:04:58,412 --> 00:04:59,577
Exam level now.

119
00:04:59,577 --> 00:05:01,453
Which of these is aromatic?

120
00:05:01,453 --> 00:05:02,668
Pause and solve.

121
00:05:08,068 --> 00:05:09,138
The answer is B.

122
00:05:09,138 --> 00:05:10,698
The cycloheptatrienyl cation.

123
00:05:11,198 --> 00:05:14,921
It is a flat ring of seven
carbons with three double bonds.

124
00:05:14,921 --> 00:05:16,136
Six pi electrons.

125
00:05:16,636 --> 00:05:21,229
The positive carbon has an empty p
orbital, so the ring of p orbitals is

126
00:05:21,229 --> 00:05:22,129
complete.

127
00:05:21,980 --> 00:05:23,817
Six fits four n plus two.

128
00:05:24,317 --> 00:05:26,100
A and D have four pi electrons.

129
00:05:26,100 --> 00:05:27,763
C has eight, and is a tub.

130
00:05:28,363 --> 00:05:29,904
Here is your recap.

131
00:05:29,904 --> 00:05:31,488
Screenshot this card.

132
00:05:34,688 --> 00:05:36,874
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

133
00:05:37,274 --> 00:05:38,174
One.

134
00:05:37,733 --> 00:05:41,442
Why are all six carbon carbon
bonds in benzene the same length?

135
00:05:42,242 --> 00:05:43,142
Two.

136
00:05:42,583 --> 00:05:44,363
What are the conditions of Huckel's rule?

137
00:05:45,163 --> 00:05:46,063
Three.

138
00:05:45,690 --> 00:05:49,076
Which gas goes through a red
hot iron tube to make benzene?

139
00:05:49,976 --> 00:05:54,568
Next time: how does benzene swap a
hydrogen without breaking its ring?

140
00:05:54,568 --> 00:05:57,291
We find out in electrophilic substitution.
